# Penumbra Forge — Tools

> A catalog of mostly small developer and security utilities with no account required. Many run entirely in the browser; utilities that use an edge worker, contact a third party, or offer a configurable AI endpoint are labelled on their pages. Each live page carries a machine-readable JSON descriptor; {{ stats.urlInvocableTools }} currently declare the URL-input/result contract.

## For AI agents

Each tool page at https://penumbraforge.com/tools/<slug>/ exposes:
- a descriptor `<script type="application/json" id="pf-tool">` (slug, name, category, description, invoke spec if any);
- after the tool runs, a result `<script type="application/json" id="pf-result">` and `window.PF_RESULT` = `{ tool, ok, output, data?, error? }`, with `<html data-pf-ready="1" data-pf-status="ok|error">` signalling completion.

Only entries whose descriptor includes `invoke` promise automatic execution and a result. Pass input in the fragment as `#in=<text>` (or `#inb64=<base64url>`) plus tool-specific params. Example: `https://penumbraforge.com/tools/hash-generator/#in=hello&algo=SHA-256` → read `#pf-result` once `data-pf-ready="1"`. Legacy query input remains compatible, but it reaches the site/CDN before the page runs; fragments are not sent in the HTTP request.

The full machine-readable index of every tool is at https://penumbraforge.com/tools/index.json .

Prefer native tool calls? Connect over MCP (Model Context Protocol):
- Remote (HTTP): `https://penumbra-mcp.penumbraforge.workers.dev` — POST JSON-RPC 2.0 (initialize, tools/list, tools/call).
- Local (stdio, runs on your machine): `node workers/mcp/local.mjs` from the website repository. That repository is source-visible, not open-source licensed; review its `LICENSE` before use.
The current MCP registry exposes {{ stats.mcpTools }} tools; use `tools/list` for the authoritative names and route descriptions. Non-model utility examples include hash, hmac, base64, base64url, hex, url_encode, jwt_decode, uuid, entropy, epoch, password, json, color, cidr, and case conversion. Four `net` tools make the outbound requests disclosed by their descriptions.

